hi this is Daniel Dwyer from GameSpot  reading the words of reviewer Austin  Walker project cars doesn't have  unlockables you don't amass a feat of  high-performance vehicles you don't buy  any new drive trains or earn prototype  engines you can choose to work your way  through a long career of tiered racing  events but even this is optional you can  just as easily start at the highest race  class with the best cars facing the  toughest competition rajae cars doesn't  deliver a fantasy of accumulation and  progress what it does deliver is a  simulation of racing as tense as it is  deep  this simulation is rolled out across  four modes the solo race and online  modes let you build any race you'd like  from the set of cars tracks motorsport  restrictions realism settings and  weather conditions available while the  range of vehicles is why there are some  notable absences if you're desperate to  drive Ferrari Porsche or Honda for  instance project cars isn't going to  meet that need but the cars that are  available look appropriately gorgeous on  learning each vehicles intricacies  offers a singular pleasure holding the  reins of a growling Ford Mustang is  widely different from zipping around the  truck and a little super cart  career mode puts you in the role of a  driver as you move from one tier to  another signing contracts with new teams  earning new sponsorship and going after  self-chosen goals and achievements a  range of Motorsports is available with  low tiers devoted to karts the middle  tier is offering some open wheel races  and GT events and the top tier is  introducing prototype racing series and  the final mode is driver network which  offers players the chance to compete  against each other in an asynchronous  time trial challenge winning means not  just racing well but by understanding  how to tune your car to better fit the  races tracking conditions there are  dozens of characteristics for you to  learn about adjust and test out change  how open your radiator is modify your  gear timing increase or reduce tire  pressure and even manage your fuel load  each of these has some sort of effect on  your driving and if you really hope to  compete  you can't take a one-size-fits-all  approach you'll need to learn what all  of these options mean unfortunately  unless you already know the ins and outs  of car tuning this means that you'll  need to seek help outside of the game  the bottom of the tuning screen says you  can troubleshoot issues by asking your  engineer but there's not actually a way  to do that in the game short  descriptions of the tuning options give  you a broad idea but further specifics  you'll need to turn to fan-made guides  and tutorial videos this is a problem  for project cars because it makes  approaching the game on its own terms  much harder it doesn't help that the AI  racers in career and solo modes are so  inconsistent with the default difficulty  settings the other drivers vary between  smartly aggressive and totally passive  why should you spend an hour learning  how to master the line of sonoma raceway  if you can win by a margin of 12 seconds  even when you don't put time into  studying the track couple this with a  rare but very annoying bug that randomly  switches you from first to last place  and the whole experience can get a  little bit sour  the multiplayer mode sidestepped the  problem of unpredictable AI and it's in  the online competition the project cars  shines brightest online it all comes  together it's the last lap and you're  fighting for space against a group of  ten other real-life players you pull  ahead and suddenly you're neck and neck  for first place going into the final  straightaway you notice it before your  rival does his car just isn't keeping up  with juries after the race is over and  everyone is congratulating everyone else  on a good race your rival says something  like man I don't know what happened  there it's like the car just gave out on  me and though you can't be sure you  think you know that it's because of how  much fuel was left in the tank you  tested that during the practice session  and that work had paid off  that's project cars at its best no  experience points no parts to buy no  cars to add to your collection the  audacious decision to offer everything  upfront it forms the rest of project  cars design making it distinct and  sometimes frustrating other games in the  genre work like Skinner boxes offering  rewards according to a special schedule  designed to keep the player hooked these  games offer a fantasy of plotting  constant accumulation or low stakes  action project cars offers a different  fantasy one that's a little less  attractive and a bit harder to enjoy the  fantasy of learning had to do something  difficult  you
 

